"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,361\nThe Learning Brain. Series 2: Memory\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,225\nEpisode 8\n\n3\n00:00:07,305 --> 00:00:09,433\nReconsolidation\n\n4\n00:00:12,233 --> 00:00:14,002\nEach time you recall a memory,\n\n5\n00:00:14,022 --> 00:00:15,906\nit may become open to change.\n\n6\n00:00:16,274 --> 00:00:18,656\nRats learned that a tone meant a shock.\n\n7\n00:00:18,973 --> 00:00:21,246\nA day later, the tone was played again,\n\n8\n00:00:21,412 --> 00:00:25,487\nand a drug that blocks protein synthesis was put into the amygdala.\n\n9\n00:00:25,931 --> 00:00:27,791\nThe next day, the fear was gone.\n\n10\n00:00:28,182 --> 00:00:31,120\nWithout the reminder, the same drug did nothing.\n\n11\n00:00:31,537 --> 00:00:32,757\nGiven six hours later,\n\n12\n00:00:32,777 --> 00:00:34,168\nit did nothing either.\n\n13\n00:00:34,338 --> 00:00:36,774\nSo retrieval does not just read a memory.\n\n14\n00:00:37,094 --> 00:00:38,133\nIt can unlock it.\n\n15\n00:00:38,596 --> 00:00:43,247\nThe engram from episode six becomes unstable again for a few hours,\n\n16\n00:00:43,401 --> 00:00:45,190\nand then has to be stored again.\n\n17\n00:00:45,725 --> 00:00:47,698\nThis is reconsolidation.\n\n18\n00:00:47,868 --> 00:00:49,151\nDoes it happen in people?\n\n19\n00:00:49,449 --> 00:00:52,122\nStudents learned a set of objects on day one.\n\n20\n00:00:52,581 --> 00:00:56,338\nOn day two, some were reminded of that session in the same room,\n\n21\n00:00:56,442 --> 00:00:57,852\nthen learned a second set.\n\n22\n00:00:58,411 --> 00:01:00,801\nOn day three, asked for the first set,\n\n23\n00:01:01,043 --> 00:01:03,960\nthe reminded group mixed in objects from the second.\n\n24\n00:01:04,479 --> 00:01:06,836\nWithout the reminder, they rarely did.\n\n25\n00:01:07,162 --> 00:01:09,763\nAnd the mixing only showed up after a delay.\n\n26\n00:01:09,933 --> 00:01:11,721\nIt may also allow updating.\n\n27\n00:01:12,100 --> 00:01:14,408\nPeople learned to fear a coloured square.\n\n28\n00:01:14,813 --> 00:01:19,348\nA reminder ten minutes before extinction training stopped the fear from returning.\n\n29\n00:01:19,735 --> 00:01:22,255\nA reminder six hours before did not.\n\n30\n00:01:22,684 --> 00:01:25,790\nIn those retested, the effect lasted a year.\n\n31\n00:01:25,960 --> 00:01:27,850\nOne condition seems to matter.\n\n32\n00:01:28,220 --> 00:01:32,531\nA memory is only opened if something about the reminder is unexpected.\n\n33\n00:01:32,934 --> 00:01:34,602\nWithout that prediction error,\n\n34\n00:01:34,631 --> 00:01:35,994\nit stays locked.\n\n35\n00:01:36,164 --> 00:01:36,964\nA caution.\n\n36\n00:01:37,392 --> 00:01:38,978\nHuman results are mixed.\n\n37\n00:01:39,352 --> 00:01:44,051\nFour direct replications of a well known motor skill study found no effect,\n\n38\n00:01:44,253 --> 00:01:47,161\nand some fear studies have not replicated either.\n\n39\n00:01:47,669 --> 00:01:50,113\nWhether old memories are rewritten,\n\n40\n00:01:50,228 --> 00:01:52,962\nor simply outcompeted, is still debated.\n\n41\n00:01:53,132 --> 00:01:53,946\nFor teaching.\n\n42\n00:01:54,378 --> 00:01:57,777\nBring out students' existing ideas before you correct them.\n\n43\n00:01:58,208 --> 00:02:00,552\nThen show something that surprises them.\n\n44\n00:02:00,908 --> 00:02:02,792\nAnd when two topics are similar,\n\n45\n00:02:02,970 --> 00:02:04,658\nmark the differences clearly,\n\n46\n00:02:04,740 --> 00:02:08,256\nsince reactivated memories can absorb new material.\n\n47\n00:02:08,426 --> 00:02:10,794\nRemembering is not just reading a memory.\n\n48\n00:02:11,111 --> 00:02:12,686\nIt opens it for editing.\n\n"
